//
// A scalar byte-store loop must store every byte.
//
// The pinned Peano (llvm-aie 21.0.0.2026080301+c9c5ecb7) miscompiles this. It
// unrolls the loop by 4 and the last store of each iteration ends up at the
// zero-overhead-loop boundary, executing once after the loop instead of once per
// iteration -- so 769 of 1024 bytes are written and the missing ones are exactly
// those at i % 4 == 3. Silently: no warning, no fault, just stale bytes.
//
// The unroll factor decides it, and the count of missing bytes is (1024/UF) - 1:
// correct at 1 and 8, 511 wrong at 2, 255 wrong at 4, which is the compiler's
// default choice here. int32 and int64 stores are unaffected, as is a volatile
// destination.
//
// Fixed upstream in llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026082201+63a871b0, so this is XFAIL rather
// than a bug report: when utils/peano-requirements.txt moves to a nightly with
// the fix, lit reports XPASS and forces this file to be updated. That is the
// point of writing it this way -- a hand-maintained note about a known-bad
// toolchain outlives the bug by years, and nothing makes anyone revisit it.
//
// Kept out of the overlay suite deliberately: this is a property of the
// toolchain, not of overlays. It is here because the overlay work is what found
// it -- a memset-style byte loop was leaving every fourth byte of a slot
// uncleared, which looked for a long time like a hardware race.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
// XFAIL: *
//
// RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
// RUN: %PEANO_INSTALL_DIR/bin/clang++ --target=aie2p-none-unknown-elf -O2 -c %S/kernel.cc -o %t/kernel.o
// RUN: %python %S/aie2.py --dev npu2 --out %t/design.mlir
// RUN: cd %t && %aiecc --tmpdir=%t/tmp --get-xclbin --xclbin-name=%t/aie.xclbin --get-npu-insts --npu-insts-name=%t/insts.bin %t/design.mlir
// RUN: %host_clang %S/test.cpp -o %t/test.exe -std=c++17 -Wall %xrt_flags %host_link_flags %test_utils_flags
// RUN: %t/test.exe -x %t/aie.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i %t/insts.bin | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: all 1024 bytes stored
// CHECK: PASS!
